Date: Tue, 29 Jun 1993 00:27:18 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: The Game of "Go" as an Example

T. David Burns:
> Several persons have noted that a GA may not produce a Go champ due to
> genetic drift or a weak initial population.

Genetic drift can be treated by increasing the population and/or
having more elitist selection (ie giving more fit strings more than linear 
fitness scores, eg ensuring the top scorer is preserved).  You pointed 
to one of several solutions for achieving proper code size and diversity, 
by allowing reasonably open-ended expansion of the code size (within 
reasonable time/space constraints).  

Time performance is an interesting factor.  Computers were able to beat 
human chess masters at speed-chess long before normal chess.  I suspect 
the same will be true with Go, since the fitness function (the time it 
takes to play a game) will dominate the time it takes to evolve good Go 
playing programs, good quick programs will tend to be evolved before
better slow ones.

> There is also the question of
> the encoding scheme. 

This is not a big deal.  Either Lisp S-expressions or binary strings 
can encode arbitrary Turing machines.  Although for most 
practical/engineering problems, we'll want to "cheat" and start 
with relatively high-level building blocks, or other constraints on the 
encoding scheme (eg insisting on game trees for games, confining ourselves 
to shape trees for parts-design problems, etc.)

Careful: introducing such building blocks or constraints may preclude
the evolution of building blocks superior to the preexisting ones.  
Especially in a game like Go, for which human strategies are relatively 
vague and recently learned, I would make the encoding as open-ended as 
possible, even at major cost in short-term learning performance.  In 
particular, building in pre-conceived meta-strategies like game trees 
is a bad idea, IMHO.

>[co-evolution provides an ever-shifting landscape]
> Go has no
> stable mountaintop, no equilibrium. (I think.)

Correct.  Furthermore, I suspect finding a "global optimum" for most 
complex multi-player games is equivalent to trying to solve the halting 
problem, because part of the problem is trying to predict what your 
opponent will do (and vice versa).   This reinforces the point
Tim May and I have made, that effective GA/GP, and most practical
learning in general, is satisficing not optimization.  A thousand
years from now, Jupiter-sized brains will likely still be evolving
better Go strategies than their forebears.

>>Tim May: [all strategy information inherent in the rules of Go]

> I'm not sure. The rules of the game could be encoded briefly, but some
> meta-programming needs to be done. 

But the meta-programming can be limited to implementing a general-purpose
genetic algorithm, a general-purpose Turing machine interpreter (this
is assumed by AIT anyway), and encoding the game rules in binary
action/message terms.  In some cases introducing building blocks
by hand, or constraining the search space in some way (eg by insisting
that the Go programs use a game tree) will increase the search
efficiency, in some cases it won't.  Also, there might be a trade-off
between evolving good players in the short term by starting with 
known successful strategies, and in the long term by giving the GA
freedom to search across the entire solution space of TMs that
play Go.

> [why can't]
> the brain generate the best strategy just from knowing the rules and
> looking at the board? Our tape is noisy?

There is probably no such thing as the "best" strategy (see above), 
but many brains in parallel have generated quite good strategies over
the years, by doing just what the GA-encoded playing programs would
be doing, playing games with each other.  But, "good" relative to 
what?  Once we let GAs rome freely over the space of Go-playing TMs, 
we'll having something to compare ourselves to!

