Date: Tue, 29 Jun 1993 02:26:34 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

With Tim May's posting of the memory (algorithmic complexity) vs.
time (logical depth) space, and now the notion of "critical bits",
etc. the seminar gets interesting!  Much thanks also to 
Tim for generously OCRing the AIT stuff.

Tim writes:
> [gas & Derek Zahn's frog-thru-the-blender:
>  no critical bits, no "structure" or fitness for a function]

The concept of "critical bits", which have major impact on overall 
fitness if removed, strongly resembles the GA-theory concept of 
"schemata".  A schema uses the string's alphabet plus * for "don't
care".  For example, three queens in a five-card hand might be "Q*QQ**", 
and we say that this schema has an order of 3 in a string of length 5.  
The poker example makes for good explanation; GA theory differs in
that order matters and the strings are binary.

In a population N of binary strings of length L, we have between
2^L and N2^L schemata, depending on popuation diversity.
In GA short schemata, or "building blocks", tend to increase or decrease 
in the population exponentially with their fitness relative to
other schemata.  Long or widely separated schemata tend to be broken 
up by crossover and don't increase exponentially.  (What constitutes
"short" and "long" depends on the specifics of how crossover is
done, string length L, etc.)  

The plot of fitness vs. time for GA or GP roughly follows the "learning" 
or "logistics" curve; fitness tends to be O(log(t)) in the
long run but in the short run it bounces around quite a bit, depending
on the topology of the space and crossover rate as a function of
fitness.  As GA hops between hills in fitness space, higher mountains 
become fewer and far between, until (if this is a small search space) 
we're on top of K-2 sampling the space randomly for those higher 
points on Everest, or vice versa.  (BTW, did they ever decide which of 
those mountains is higher after all?)  The probability of breaking
up a string on one hilltop to create a string on higher ground
I call the "editing distance".  

When climbing a hill, or with nearby higher hills
around, it is easy to find the higher points (except for those
rare problems that are "deceptive", eg a bunch of equally sized
small hills and one tall thin beanstalk into space :-)  But
once we reach the high peaks, sampling the even taller peaks
becomes tougher and tougher, until it becomes prohibitvely 
improbable.  

We might measure criticality by editing-distance'/fitness',
ie how little editing it takes to lower a string's fitness
by how much.  A string with long, highly fit schemata will be very 
easy to destroy; a string with a bunch of redundant little
building blocks will be tougher.

Going the other direction, editing-distance'/fitness' is a measure
of stability -- how much editing it takes to increase fitness by
how much.  If we hold a garbage poker hand it takes very little
editing; with a straight it takes a highly improbable amount
of editing to get to a royal flush.  (If I've lost anybody, I'll
rehash the poker example again below).

"Punctuated equlibrium" occurs when the top GA scorer has
been sitting around one hilltop for a while, and after a
period of fruitless sampling finds a higher piece of ground.  
That higher piece of ground is probably not a local optimum, so
the GA will hill-climb for a while, or there might be some
other interested stuff.  Stephen Jay Gould saw these 
stasis/rapid evolution cycles in the fossil record, but it
wasn't explained rigorously until GA and similar mathematical
models of evolution appeared. (BTW, Chaitin needs to study this
stuff, instead of coming out and saying "nobody has yet created
a mathematical model of evolution", which is silly.  But I don't
wish to single out Chaitin; he's just one of many making such
mistakes in our over-specialized age).

So there is still room for pessimists to argue that the
self-reproduction of bacteria, intelligence of brains, etc. are 
"too complex", by arguing that they are made up of very long schemata 
that cannot be easily broken down into smaller building blocks.  However, 
considering the short period intelligence evolved from simpler building 
blocks (c. 3 million years, or 300,000 generations, typical of a 
several-day GP run on today's computers), and self-reproduction evolved 
from autocatalytic sets (< 300 million years) argues against this.  Cf. 
William Calvin's theory about how language and music evolved from the 
simple building-blocks of sequencing for stone-throwing, for example.  
Humans and chimps share c. 99.5% of our genomes, so the editing 
distance from low to high IQ is fairly small.  (This is verified
by the experience of AI, which has had an easier time automating
high-IQ SAT-style skills than animal/robot style skills).

For bacterial reproduction, the issue is not how much complexity was 
created during genetic evolution, but how much was created _before_
genetic evolution, by the "computation" of autocatalytic sets
and the improbability of the origin of life.  (A royal flush is no
less complex if obtained in the deal than if obtained after
several draws).

Areas where tech has already surpassed life are probably more "logically 
shallow", ie their design can be composed from short building blocks: 
flying, swimming, surface locomotion, most practical mathematics, etc.
On the other hand, it may be that the schemata for intelligence 
and self-rep are not much longer, but instead a larger editing 
distance away from the current design/engineering/science memes of
our civilizations.  The latter possibility is why I'm excited about
using GP to tackle the complexity of new search spaces off the
beaten track in civilization's design space (what Tim calls
"k-space").

>[royal flush -- improbable but important point in poker hand space]
> [whether one mutates hand by drawing depends on what one already
>  has]

> Holding a flush or a straight is "maximally complex" 

The royal flush is indeed the Mount Everest of poker.  (Or
should that be the K-2 of poker? :-)

One might try drawing from a 9-10-J-Q-K flush anyway, to get
from that flush to the superior royal flush.  Whether this
is a good idea depends on (a) what costs are there to drawing,
and (b) what is the editing distance from the current hand
to a better hand.  In this case, the editing distance is
one, and the probability of drawing Ace of
suite is 1/47 (with 5 cards in the hand and no jokers).
To go from a straight to a flush the editing distance is at least 
four, with extreme intermediate degradation in fitness of the hand, 
since we have to break up the straight schemata in order to achieve 
