Subject: A Rand Fan's Reply

In answer to the question about how a Rand fan would view immortalism in
light of the argument that ethics are contingent upon the conditional
nature of life, and that immortal, indestructible robots wouldn't need
any ethics:

I view immortalism simply as analogous to mathematical limits like
infinity.  You can approach them, but you can never get there.  You can
get closer and closer, but you can't reach the terminus.  Eternal life
is the limit to longevity, but all we can do is live longer and longer.
We can't live eternally, but we can die trying!

Contrary to Perry, I don't think that this is one of Rand's mistakes.  I
think she made different mistakes.  Also note that Ludwig von Mises argues
in Human Action that perfect, immortal beings wouldn't even act at all,
much less act ethically.

