  Okay, as the only licensed electrician on the list that I know of
I'll add my 2-bits.
  
   The way it was portrayed in the movie, the kid was safe till he
stepped off the fence.

 Electric fences require a ground, in a normal installation
(cattle) the strands are electrified, and contact with the ground
supplies the return. It is possible to electrify some strands and
have alternate strands provide the ground, but if you remember
there were wires interconnecting the strands....i.e. the strands
were all electrified.

 Stepping off the fence was the dangerous part, if you remember the
fence was on top of a concrete wall about three foot high. This was
still very dangerous, since concrete is porous and they had just
faced a serious downpour. I have seen many people shocked while
standing on concrete.

 Just for further info;

 The normal voltage for an electric fence is about 11,000 volts 
(DC) at about an amp or so. In a high impedance fence this DC
voltage is pulsed at about 4,000 times a second, in a low impedance
fence the pulse rate is about 10,000 times a second. Remember it is
amperage that kills. In some types of fences known as "weed
burners" there is a periodic pulse of 3/4 of a second duration used
to keep the fence line clear of vegetation. The potential fire
hazard is obvious, thus this type is banned in many areas.



