Alexander Chislenko says:
>    It looks like veganism wasn't very popular among pre-industrial cultures
> (or any other).  Are Jains the only example of a vegan culture?

Well, I'm not sure, but possibly. Most pre-industrial cultures are
largely vegan in the sense that they don't get terribly much meat
whether they want it or not and in the sense that most of the world's
peoples are lactose intolerant. However, I don't know of many cultures
that intentionally abstain from eating meat. Remember that until about
a century or so ago this wasn't a health issue -- most people couldn't
afford to eat much meat.

Anyway, as I've said many times, there is nothing magic about eating
NO meat -- its just that you should likely eat little enough that for
practical purposes you are going to be a vegan. Given that, I find its
easier just to go all the way, since that way I don't have to keep
track of my food consumption and can just follow simple rules to stay
healthy. If you feel like having some chicken once a month, I doubt
its going to do you that much harm -- but this is so much less meat
consumption than most people are used to that its almost unimportant
compared to the general point.

