Date: Tue, 29 Jun 93 10:03:02 -0700
From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Flying snakes

Several people have commented on the utility or lack of it of the gliding
adaptation of the paradise tree snake of Borneo.  Since the critter makes
a living munching on things found in treetops (I suspect it is a carnivore
but cannot cite a reference), the ability to get from one treetop to the
next one without going all the way down to the ground and back up is a
likely win for several reasons:  First, it saves time -- less travel time
works out to more hunting/eating time.  Second, it saves metabolic energy.
Third, it saves exposure to ground-dwelling predators.  Fourth, it may
provide a means of escape from arboreal predators.

I have read speculation that insects developed flight via an evolutionary
path that started with insects that fed above ground level in plants simply
jumping off to get back to the ground.  Terminal velocity for a small insect
is of course nowhere near fatal, and when you consider that the view down
many plant stems is studded with branches, leaves, twigs, thorns and what
have you, all pointing generally up, it is actually rather complicated
navigation for an insect to find a path down a plant stem; likely much
harder than finding one that goes up.

